-- | Test-framework interpretation providing an OBSERVABLE `emit` SigOp, used by
-- TraceSpec to observe a program's effect trace at runtime. Unlike the shipped
-- `I.Test.Emit` (a runtime nop), this one's x86_64 implementation writes each
-- emitted byte to stdout, so the emitted sequence — its order and arguments —
-- is observable. `exit` is NOT defined here; trace programs take it from the
-- real `I.Linux.Syscalls` (the Linux interpretation is symlinked into this test
-- strata so a single --strata root resolves both imports).
signature emit : Eff Int Unit

-- | The same for a `Float`, and OBSERVABLE for the same reason.
--
-- BOTH write the argument's WHOLE MACHINE WORD, little-endian — 8 bytes on a
-- 64-bit target, 4 on x86-32. A test that reads those bytes is observing
-- exactly what D114 says the trace carries: the SigOp's argument at its own
-- base type, not a projection of it.
--
-- `emit` used to write only the low byte, which made `emit 42` and `emit 298`
-- the same trace — a bug corrupting an argument's high bits would have passed.
-- That is the same defect D114 found in the spec, one layer down in the test
-- harness, and it is fixed here for the same reason.
--
-- The two implementations are now IDENTICAL, which is D113 showing through: at
-- the machine level an `Int` argument and a `Float` argument are both one
-- machine word. What differs is the SOURCE type, and hence what the word
-- MEANS — `42` against `0x3FE0000000000000` for `0.5` at binary64.
signature emitF : Eff Float Unit
